# Behavioral test for the explicit `'atomic` qualifier's operation mapping
# (Part 1 of the `'atomic` design — see docs/qualifiers.md's `'atomic` section):
# bare read (load), plain assignment (store), compound assign (fetch_add/fetch_sub),
# and `.swap(...)`. Runs identically under all four mode/threading combinations.
#
# `mut` (not `var`) — a scalar `'atomic` binding's every "assignment" (compound
# assign, plain store, `.swap()`) is a content-mutation through the shared
# lock-free cell, gated on `mut`/`var mut` exactly like `'actor`/`'guard`'s own
# `def`-method mutation gate; `var` alone is rebind-only, same as that family
# (docs/book.md §21's "Binding × qualifier combinations" table).
def main():
    mut counter'atomic = 0
    counter += 5
    counter -= 2
    counter = 10
    let old = counter.swap(100)
    print "{counter} {old}"

    mut flag'atomic = false
    flag = true
    print "{flag}"
